ite ovarian cancer patients  characterize healthcare access across  all five dimensions  and then look at how those are related  to initial treatment quality  supportive care and ultimately survival  and quality of life  for our five healthcare access  dimensions we think about it as  affordability which is related to cost  and finances availability which is the  type quality and volume of healthcare  resources  accessibility which is looking at the  location of healthcare services  accommodation which is addressing  organization of  services in relation to patient's  preferences  and acceptability which is getting our  patients attitude towards  their healthcare provider  for some examples for acceptability  those include things like reputation or  credentials for the provider  interpersonal competence empathy patient  provider communication  for accessibility those are things  related to location and distance  transportation parking accommodation  um include things like scheduling  and language accessibility wait times  affordability is including things like  insurance and copays or availability is  getting at a number of  doctors hospitals and our quality of  available resources  as we have got started on this walk um  uh to identify  and characterize healthcare um access  dimensions  one of the early things that we found  out was that there was no  validated instruments that  comprehensively assessed  all five healthcare dimensions the way  we had conceptualized them  um none of the existing instruments so  that looked at  separate aspects of the dimensions were  validated for african-americans or  hispanics  and we were worried that combining  questions or sub-scales from various  surveys  into one would likely limit the internal  validity of  our instruments without doing some  initial psychometric analysis  so to address this need we apply for an  nci supplement to our grant  to fund the initial qualitative work and  psychometric analysis  to develop a healthcare access  instrument  the other thing that we discovered early  in the in the process was that there was  no study that had comprehensively  described  healthcare access barriers or challenges  among hispanic cancer patients  even though they likely have more  significant barriers to care  including things like language  immigration papers and  migration patterns within the u.s and  it's really unclear how  healthcare access barriers might  uniquely affect the quality of treatment  and patient reported outcomes for  hispanic patients  so as part of our supplement we  requested  additional funding to recruit hispanic  ovarian cancer patients in our study  so our revised study design basically  took a step back  with focus group and cognitive  interviews to  get a sense of our patient ovarian  cancer survivor's perception  of healthcare access and what the  experiences were  and then our primary cohort was revised  to include hispanic patients  and then the goal is then to  characterize the health care access  dimensions  assess social demographic epidemiology  risk factors  collect some biospecimen and then  evaluate how those influence  they received or guideline ad hearing  treatment post treatment supportive care  and survival outcomes  for the formative work for our survey  development  we realized uh  pretty quickly that would have to do  this um iteratively  so starting from a thorough review of  the literature to identify  surveys or instruments that had assessed  aspects of healthcare access  and then using that as a base um for  which we build the rest of our survey  um to inform that process and to make  sure that we  are developing an instrument that  captures the experiences of minorities  specifically african americans and  hispanics  we proposed and developed a focus group  concept elicitation  process where we recruit  ovarian cancer survivors cancer  survivors it might include breasts  breast cancer survivors as well that are  white hispanic  or african-american and asked about  their  experience with their treatment journey  for cancer  and how they perceive healthcare access  and then using that to refine our survey  and doing multiple rounds  of focus groups and cognitive testing as  we develop new  questions and then pilot test in the  final um  survey material  so uh next i will present some results  of our conceptualization  slash focus group analysis for this our  initial plan was to have  four or five groups of cancer survivors  in the triangle area come into our  office um  and you know have a very robust lively  discussion about  healthcare access and what the  experiences were  unfortunately our planning coincided  with the start of kovid and so  once everything shut down we had to  change our plans  and make change our  design to have virtual focus groups so  all of our focus groups actually  happen over zoom we think that  that may have influenced our results  because while we were  planning to do significant outreach by  going out to support group sessions and  recruiting patients from our cancer  institute um  it became um it became difficult to  to do the outreach work and so we had to  rely on  people that were already plugged into  cancer support groups  and those tended to be people that were  more of higher socioeconomic status  somewhat technologically savvy and could  figure out and navigate their way around  zoom um zoom conferencing so it's likely  that our focus groups  are represents a higher ses group than  average but we did have eight for  focus groups that are occurred over the  course of  about six weeks uh the first two groups  who are all african-american  cancer survivors residing in rural north  carolina  the next three groups are part of a  sister's network  support group which is breast and  ovarian cancer patients in raleigh and  durham  and those were also mostly  african-american survivors  we also had hispanic  cancer support group and a separate  group of nationally recruited cancer  support group  participants as well as two cancer  institution navigators  as we we recorded uh all the focus group  interactions  and uh those recordings were transcribed  for the hispanic focus group that was  actually facilitated in spanish and that  was translated before being  transcribed the transcripts were all  reviewed fully to  to become familiar with the data and  their recoding scheme  was was applied using codes that we had  initially  identified and then those who are  reviewed by the study team  we then produced cross-tabulated results  for each of the healthcare access  dimensions  and identified any new emergent codes  some of the codes um that were  defined are listed down here um and  and operationalized as uh factors that  support treatment journey and those that  negatively impact treatment  in terms of the overall um our results  of our healthcare access dimension  coding  we found that of the five healthcare  access dimensions are approved during  the focus groups  acceptability was the one that was  mentioned by all seven focus groups and  it was mentioned  um uh by far um you know  way more than each of the other  dimensions so  this this uh issues related to  acceptability came up about 41  of the time um next was our  accommodation which also  was discussed in our focus groups which  came up about 20  of the time and  um as we we assessed each  um five healthcare actions i mentioned  and asked patients  about things like what do you think  what do you what comes to mind when you  think about access to care or  if you had a choice in care what are  kind of the features  that you would be interested in or what  were the challenges that you encountered  and as indicated by the dark shades  accept  issues around acceptability showed up  regardless of how the question was asked  um again should lending support that  this was one of the top  uh factors on on cancer survivors  minds and then in terms of imagine codes  um we found that in terms of codes that  were supportive of  positive treatment experiences that  having a support system  was mentioned in all the groups and was  mentioned by far the most  at times and  in terms of codes that were challenges  that were negatively associated with  treatment  we found that our fear was the number  one  issue that patients have reported  so i will describe a few of the quotes  that came up during the  focus groups associated with each of the  healthcare access dimensions  so regarding affordability patients  experiences  included you know a participant that had  to leave work because of a diagnosis  and was fortunate to sign up for  medicaid and then had their husbands  insurance that took over  um and a separate participant whose  family went down to one income because  of the diagnosis  and because they were so sick they could  not help physically at home  making the first year of the diagnosis  being the most difficult  we also had a participant that took a  leave of absence and had insurance that  took  care of all their financial um  needs um and their copay was was  manageable  as well so we see a wide spectrum of  experiences related to affordability  regarding availability we also see a  wide  range of experiences from one  participant who said  the first question was where do i go my  um  do i go to my primary care provider or  my specialist  and not being sure what to do next just  called the hospital mainline  and was eventually connected to a  primary care group and an ob gyn group  for their treatment  um in contrast to another participant  who  already had a long-term relationship  with their primary care provider  and asked him to lead them through the  process  regarding accessibility um  an issue that came up with our rural  participants  was transportation and you know they  described having a county transportation  system  but because the boss left early in the  morning are  returned late at night regardless of the  time of treatment  that also became a challenge and another  participant who  indicated that they were hearing i i  am hearing i will be sick with nausea  and with all the appointments being told  someone has to be with you um who is  going to take me there being single  can't have people losing paychecks to  take me to an appointment  and so this was really a a challenge in  terms of being able to  go to their apartment appointments um  needing to find someone that would take  them  and stay with them throughout the whole  appointment and needing to work with  that person's schedule  in order to um to make the appointment  regarding accommodation um we heard from  several participants that talked about  um liking their surgeon skills but not  liking the wait times  taking up to an hour and a half or up to  six  hours and the wait time before studying  treatment  was mentioned as a very scary period  and someone mentioned needing to always  come in prepared because it was an  all-day appointment  regarding acceptability we there were  several quotes here and again this was  the top  um issue that participants mentioned  some of the quotes included a  relationship with my provider makes all  the treatments  and the name or reputation of the  institution is not a big factor  many black patients who do not  understand what is being told to them  there's a fear and mistrust of doctors  they don't see enough minority doctors  that they can relate to  even if doctors don't realize that  patients do an unconscious bias is a big  issue  and there was a participant that thought  there was some unconscious bias  because once they found out they were  educated they were treated better  um there are also participants that  talked about  more into went into more detail about  unconscious bias  you know they are telling doctors uh  white doctors you're biased you're  unconsciously biased when it comes to  dealing with minorities  and the discussion is made um you've got  to stop it  um so and it's unconscious and giving  seminars and courses that's helping to  improve the issue  and as a patient stepping in with the  attitude of  you're not putting me into a category  you're dealing with me justin  um as a follow-up to that another  participant um  said it doesn't matter to me as long as  you respect me  because really i think i really think  the discussion about caring  just means that you're feeling that if  someone cares about me  they are going to give me their best  work and that's the bottom line you want  the best work  and if you care about me you're going to  give me your best i think there's a  relationship between do they care  do they have good bedside manners you  relate that to getting quality care  of course that may not always be true  but that's what you're relating it to  in response to the question does the  risks of the provider matter are this  participant responded  no not at all um and like justine said  it's just how you treat me you know  respectfully  you can be a sister referring to being  an african-american doctor  and if your attitude is bad i don't have  anything for you so yes it does not  matter at all to me  as a follow-up to that even sex doesn't  matter to me  and that's participants um describe that  they are most of their team  were men um or female and they hadn't  had  a male doctor but that would not have  made a difference  it's the qualifications and how you  treat it and again  the level of compassion you have um  so i mentioned towards here that the  care is full circle it's emotional  it's physical and it's all of that put  together  um so just um again as as  i've presented with the quotes and the  data from the focus group coding  showing that you know first uh  participants understand  that healthcare access is not just one  thing  so it's not just about the finances or  the insurance  or the accessibility and being close to  a you know a highly ranked cancer  institute  but do you understand that there are  other dimensions that i play  uh things related to acceptability and  accommodation i've been able to make  appointments i've been able to  get to the hospital and the wait times  all showed up as being  important for patients however what um  really struck out was that even though  patients  understood the um kind of differences  in quality and the difference between a  cancer a comprehensive cancer center and  a community clinic  the issue of acceptability was what  often stuck with them  long term and generally overshadowed  their perception  of other aspects of their health care  and what kept showing up time and time  again was that  things related to comfort empathy  respect  and communication were the aspects that  overrode  other aspects of healthcare access for  patients  my this suggests that really paying  attention to creating psychologically  safe  environments for patients especially  when they are receiving a new diagnosis  or the beginning of treatment  should really be considered as a  cornerstone of patient-centered  high-quality care  we also found other facilitators and  barriers that  can interact with our aspects of  healthcare access  so having a strong support system seem  to be  a key factor having family members i  would go with them to appointments and  help them understand what the the  provider was saying  um faith also was a strong i was a  strong  facilitator and giving them a sense of a  sense of comfort  in going through a difficult treatment  journey  fear came up as something that would be  a barrier to  um accessing care and receiving quality  treatment  in terms of holding people back from  wanting to  engage with providers or wanting to go  in out of fear of getting the bad news  um and also mental and emotional  awareness you know just um being psycho  psychologically ready to go through this  the other factor that came up several  times was a role conflict  where for participants that were used to  taking care of people  and were usually the um  the main source of support for their  family now needing help themselves and  needing to  accept that role as seemed also to be a  barrier to care  so in summary um it appears that you  know healthcare access is definitely a  complex  um construct it has multiple dimensions  um each one of the dimensions are  important however  um in terms of the patient provider  interaction this seems to have  the most impact on patients  satisfaction with their care and just  their psychological safety  during the course of their cancer  treatment  in terms of creating patient centered  high quality care  this is actually one um dimension of  healthcare access that might be easier  to intervene on  by providing training for providers and  not just the oncologist but the entire  healthcare team  to be able to provide this empathetic  respectful  healthcare system for patients  so with that i would just like to  acknowledge  the co-investigators and trainees and  staff that have contributed to this work  and also to acknowledge funding by the  national cancer institute  thank you
 

